<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
  <section xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-03-farming-ship">
    <title>Farming Ship - The Player</title>
    <introduction>
    <p>
        The Player is a farming ship in space. For now, we are using a
        green space ship image without any specific nod to farming. 
        A future version may have a ship that has more visible farming 
        specific parts. The farming ship will move around with thrusters,
        and for now will have some automatic damper system that slows motion
        to a stop after the thrusters stop and creates a maximum speed for the
        ship's motion.
    </p>
    
    </introduction>
    <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-farming-ship-scene">
      <title>The Player Scene</title>
      <p>
        Make a new scene. Add a RigidBody2D as the root Node, then add
        a CollisionPolygon2D and a Sprite2D similar to the collision scene 
        template shown in <xref ref="first-2d-figure-collision-scene-template-d" />
        except that we are using a CollisionPolygon2D instead of a CollisionShape2D. 
        Rename the RigidBody2D Node to Player. Make sure the scene looks like 
        <xref ref="farm-ship-initial-scene-figure" />, then save the scene.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the Sprite2D add the ship image as the Texture. Then select the
        CollisionPolygon2D and, in the Inspector, change the Build Mode to Segments.
        Then, in the 2D view, zoom the view so that the ship is fully visible and 
        mostly filling the window. Click on the top of the ship, and then work your way
        around the edge of the ship as shown in <xref ref="farm-ship-collision-drawn-figure" />.
        Save the scene.
      </p>
      <p>
        Next, in the inspector set the PhysicsMaterial in the Physics Material Override part 
        of the RigidBody2D as shown in <xref ref="farm-ship-physics-figure"/> Then in the Project Settings under Physics 2D, set Default Gravity 
        to <m>0.0 px/s^2</m> as shown in <xref ref="space-farm-gravity-figure"/>.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Setting up the Player Scene</caption> 
        <sidebyside>
      <figure xml:id="farm-ship-initial-scene-figure">
        <caption>Collision Scene Using CollisionPolygon2D</caption>
      <image source="space-farm-ship-collision-scene.webp" width="25%">
         <shortdescription>Scene Dock with an Area2D as the root with Sprite2D and CollisionPolygon2D as children.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="farm-ship-collision-drawn-figure">
        <caption>The Collision Segments around the ship.</caption>
      <image source="farm-ship-collision.webp" width="25%">
         <shortdescription>The ship image outlined with the points needed for the CollisionPolygon2D</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="farm-ship-physics-figure">
        <caption>Adding the Physics Material Override.</caption>
      <image source="space-farm-ship-physics-material.webp" width="25%">
         <shortdescription>The Inspector is shown with the PhysicsMaterial menu item for RigidBody2D</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="space-farm-gravity-figure">
        <caption>Override Default Gravity.</caption>
      <image source="space-farm-global-gravity.webp" width="25%">
         <shortdescription>Project settings are shown with default gravity to 0.0</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
    </sidebyside>
    </figure>
      <p>
        
      </p>
      </subsection>
      <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-farming-ship-input">
      <title>Player Input</title>
      <p>
        The player input should have four inputs "thrust","turn_left",
        "turn_right", and "launch". Use the Input Map in the project settings
        to do so. If you haven't done this before, then refer to 
        <xref ref="input-map-add-action-figure" /> and the context that it's in.
      </p>
      </subsection>
      <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-farming-ship-motion">
      <title>Player Motion</title>
      <p> 
        Now that the input is mapped, we can work on player motion. Create a new script
        and add the following member variables a <c>Vector2</c> for <c>thrust</c>, three
        <c>float</c> for thrust_power, rot_power, and rot_dir. A
        <c>Vector2i</c> for the <c>view_size</c> that allows the ship to wrap.
      </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
extends RigidBody2D

var thrust : Vector2 = Vector2.ZERO
var thrust_power: float = 500
var rot_power: float = 8000
var rot_dir: float = 0
var view_size : Vector2i
</program>
    <p> 
        Each time the player comes to view, it needs to setup a few things.
        First, since we are using a CollisionPolygon2D, we need to set the 
        inertia of the RigidBody2D so that it has a valid inertial value.
        Then we need to update the view size so that we can handle wrapping,
        and connect the viewport size_changed signal to the update_view_size()
        member function.
      </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
# Called when the node enters the scene tree for the first time.
func _ready() -> void:
    inertia = 300;
    update_view_size()
    get_viewport().size_changed.connect(update_view_size)
    </program>
    <p> 
        update_view_size() sets our local view size based on the visual area of the scene.
      </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
func update_view_size() -> void:
    view_size = get_viewport().get_visible_rect().size
    </program>
    <p> 
        Since, RigidBody2D is moved by the physics process, 
        motion is indirect. The <c>_process()</c> function can
        read the input and change values that are used by the 
        physics process, but input code shouldn't happen there.
        The input function will first reset motion related values
        to 0, and then use <c>Input</c> calls to update those same
        motion related variables used in the physics process. 
      </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
# Called every frame. 'delta' is the elapsed time since the previous frame.
func _process(delta: float) -> void:
    get_input()
    
func get_input() -> void:
    thrust = Vector2.ZERO
    rot_dir = 0
    
    if Input.is_action_pressed("thrust"):
        thrust = transform.x * thrust_power
    
    rot_dir = Input.get_axis("rotate_left", "rotate_right")
    </program>
    <p> 
        The physics process will update <c>constant_force</c> for translational
        forces, and <c>constant_torque</c> for rotational forces.
      </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
func _physics_process(delta: float) ->void:
    # note: we are not updateing a value over time here
    #       so delta is not used.
    constant_force = thrust
    constant_torque = rot_dir * rot_power
    </program>
    <p> 
        If you run the scene at this point, the ship should 
        move and turn based on input. However, it may be 
        difficult to control because the thrust is moving it 
        to the right instead of forward and the constant forces cause
        constant accelleration, so things can go very fast and 
        stay moving for a while. 
    </p>
    <p> To fix the direction, go to the 2D view, click on the
        Sprite2D Node. Then click on the inspector and change 
        the rotation in the Node2D Transform to 90 degrees.
        When you do this, you'll notice that the collision shape
        doesn't rotate with it. To fix that, click on the 
        CollisionPolygon2D and change the rotation in the Node2D 
        Transform to 90 degrees.
    </p>
    <p>You can make travel smoother by
        adding Damp values for both Linear and Angular in RigidBody2D.
        A Linear Damp of 1.0 and an angular damp of 5.0 will work
        pretty well, but you are welcome to experiment with the Damp 
        value that works best for you.
      </p>
      <p>
        The other thing you might notice when testing the player scene is that
        the player can leave the scene. Instead, we'd like the ship to 
        wrap around when it gets to an edge. This can't be done in process or 
        physics_process. Instead, it needs to happen in the 
        <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_rigidbody2d.html#class-rigidbody2d-private-method-integrate-forces">
            <c>_integrate_forces()</c></url> member function inherited from
        RigidBody2D.
      </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
func _integrate_forces(state: PhysicsDirectBodyState2D) -> void:
    # wrap the ship location
    var temp_transform : Transform2D = state.transform
    temp_transform.origin.x = wrapf(temp_transform.origin.x, 0, view_size.x)
    temp_transform.origin.y = wrapf(temp_transform.origin.y, 0, view_size.y)
    state.transform = temp_transform
    </program>
    <p> 
        Now that the player motion is worked out, we can add a launcher for
        the lasso, and then add some visual effects for the rocket flame.
      </p>
      </subsection>
      <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-farming-ship-launcher">
      <title>Adding the Launcher</title>
      <p>
        We want each lasso to be launched from the ship, but not necessarily 
        the center of the ship, so we will add a Marker2D Node to the Player
        and call it Launcher.
        Then, in the 2D Mode, drag the Launcher along the x-axis to the front of the ship.
        It's fine if it's a little bit in front of the ship. This Launcher will be used
        as the starting transform for each lasso Collector. 
      </p>
      <p>
        In addition to a Marker2D, we want to have a Timer to specify how long it 
        takes to load the lasso onto the launcher, because that is the amount of time
        the player will need to wait until they can launch again. So add a Timer and call
        it CollectorLoadTime. Set the One Shot and Autostart to on in the inspector.
      </p>
      <p>
        To support the timer functionality, we'll need two new member variables,
        <c>can_launch</c> and <c>launch_rate</c>. We'll export the launch rate so 
        that it's easy to change in the inspector.
      </p>
       <program language="gdscript">
var can_launch: bool = true
@export var launch_rate: float = 0.25
    </program>
    <p> 
      Now we can add the launch function as follows.
      We set can_launch to false, start the timer,
      instantiate a new lasso, add the lasso to the scene,
      and start the lasso growing from the launcher position.  
    </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
func launch() -> void:
    can_launch = false
    $CollectorLoadTime.start()
    var collector: Collector = collector_scene.instantiate()
    get_tree().root.add_child(collector)
    collector.start($Launcher.global_transform)
    </program>
    <p> 
        This will make it so launch can only happen once, since there is
        nothing to set can_launch to true. So, we need to connect the
        timeout() signal from the CollectorLoadTime timer to a new function.
    </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
func _on_collector_load_time_timeout() -> void:
    can_launch = true
    </program>
    <p> 
       Now run the scene, and the player can move around and
       launch lassos. You may want to change the timing of how 
       long it takes to load a new collector.
    </p>
      </subsection>
